By Cauchy-Schwarz,
\[(a + 2b) \left( \frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{2b} \right) \ge (1 + \sqrt{2})^2 = 3 + 2 \sqrt{2}.\]Hence,
\[\frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{b} \ge \frac{3 + 2 \sqrt{2}}{1} = 3 + 2 \sqrt{2}.\]Equality occurs when $a = 1,$ $b = \frac{1}{2},$ so the minimum value is $\boxed{3 + 2 \sqrt{2}}.$The answer is 3+2\sqrt{2}

