Then $\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to $\mathbf{a}.$  Hence,
\[\|\mathbf{v}\| = \|\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{a}\|.\]Also,
\begin{align*}
\|\mathbf{v}\| &= \left\| \frac{1}{3} \|\mathbf{b}\| \|\mathbf{c}\| \mathbf{a} \right\| \\
&= \frac{1}{3} \|\mathbf{b}\| \|\mathbf{c}\| \|\mathbf{a}\|.
\end{align*}Then
\begin{align*}
\|\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{a}\| &= \frac{1}{3} \|\mathbf{b}\| \|\mathbf{c}\| \|\mathbf{a}\| \\
&= \frac{1}{3} \|\mathbf{a}\| \|\mathbf{b}\| \|\mathbf{c}\|.
\end{align*}Since $\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{a}$ is orthogonal to $\mathbf{a},$ it is orthogonal to $\mathbf{b}.$  Hence,
\[\|\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{a}\| = \|\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b}\|.\]Then
\begin{align*}
\|\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b}\| &= \frac{1}{3} \|\mathbf{a}\| \|\mathbf{b}\| \|\mathbf{c}\| \\
&= \frac{1}{3} \|\mathbf{b}\| \|\mathbf{c}\| \|\mathbf{a}\|.
\end{align*}But
\[\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{a} \cdot \mathbf{b} = \|\mathbf{a}\| \|\mathbf{b}\| \cos \theta,\]so
\[\|\mathbf{a}\| \|\mathbf{b}\| \cos \theta = \frac{1}{3} \|\mathbf{b}\| \|\mathbf{c}\| \|\mathbf{a}\|.\]Since $\mathbf{a},$ $\mathbf{b},$ and $\mathbf{c}$ are nonzero, we can divide both sides by $\|\mathbf{a}\| \|\mathbf{b}\|,$ which gives us
\[\cos \theta = \frac{1}{3} \|\mathbf{c}\|.\]Since $\theta$ is acute, $\sin \theta = \boxed{\frac{2 \sqrt{2}}{3}}.$The answer is \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{3}

