label("$\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}$", (B + D)/2, N);
label("$1$", (A + B)/2, S);
[/asy]

The area of a parallelogram is equal to the magnitude of the cross product of its side vectors. In this case, we have
\[\|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}\| = 1.\]
We know that $\|\mathbf{u} \times \mathbf{v}\| = \|\mathbf{u}\| \|\mathbf{v}\| \sin \theta,$ so we can write
\[1 = \|\mathbf{a}\| \|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}\| \sin \theta = \|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}\| \sin \theta.\]
Since $\|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}\|$ is given as 2, this means $\sin \theta = \frac{1}{2},$ so $\theta$ is either $\boxed{30^\circ}$ or $\boxed{150^\circ}.$

Note: We can also use the fact that the magnitude of the cross product of two vectors is equal to the product of their magnitudes times the sine of the angle between them. In this case, we have
\[1 = \|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}\| = \|\mathbf{a}\| \|\mathbf{a} \times \mathbf{c}\| \sin \theta,\]
which leads to the same conclusion.The answer is 150

