solution:
The equation of the asymptotes is $y = \pm \frac{b}{a} x$.
The slope of the line $y = \frac{b}{a} x$ is $\frac{b}{a}$, so the angle this line makes with the positive $x$-axis is $\arctan \frac{b}{a}$.
Similarly, the angle the line $y = -\frac{b}{a} x$ makes with the positive $x$-axis is $\arctan \frac{b}{a}$ as well.

[asy]
unitsize(1 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, P, Q;

A = (-2,0);
B = (2,0);
C = (0,-2);
D = (0,2);
P = (1,0.5);
Q = (1,-0.5);

draw(A--B);
draw(C--D);
draw(graph(P--Q));

label("$x$", (2,0), E);
label("$y$", (0,2), N);
label("$\frac{b}{a} x$", (2,2), NE);
label("$-\frac{b}{a} x$", (-2,-2), SW);
label("$\arctan \frac{b}{a}$", (0.4,0.2));
label("$\arctan \frac{b}{a}$", (0.4,-0.2));
[/asy]

Since the angle between the asymptotes is $60^\circ$, we must have $\arctan \frac{b}{a} + \arctan \frac{b}{a} = 60^\circ$.
Then $2 \arctan \frac{b}{a} = 60^\circ$, so $\arctan \frac{b}{a} = 30^\circ$.

If $\arctan \frac{b}{a} = 30^\circ$, then $\frac{b}{a} = \tan 30^\circ = \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}$.
Thus, $\frac{a}{b} = \sqrt{3}$.

Hence, $\frac{a}{b} = \boxed{\sqrt{3}}$.The answer is \sqrt{3}

